Building the Debian package
===========================

This file is not installed into the binary package; `README.source` is the
conventional name for instructions aimed at whoever builds the source package.

What this is, and is not
------------------------

It is a recipe kept in the upstream tree, under `contrib/`, so that it is
maintained beside the install set it claims to install. Rationale:
`docs/design/packaging.md` §6.3 — a recipe that lives only in a distribution's
own tree drifts from the install set with nothing reporting it.

It is not an official Debian package. No ITP has been filed, nobody upstream
maintains one, and `debian/control`'s `Maintainer:` is a deliberately
undeliverable placeholder. Replace it before uploading anywhere.

Rolling the orig tarball
------------------------

Build from `v0.4.2` or later, the first tag that carries this recipe. The
commands below use `v0.4.3`, the first tag with a published release.

    git archive --prefix=xtgeoip-0.4.3/ -o ../xtgeoip_0.4.3.orig.tar.gz v0.4.3
    tar xf ../xtgeoip_0.4.3.orig.tar.gz
    cd xtgeoip-0.4.3
    cp -r contrib/debian debian
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

Or take the tarball from the GitHub release, which is this same `git archive`
output, and verify it before renaming it to the orig name:

    gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
    mv xtgeoip-0.4.3.tar.gz xtgeoip_0.4.3.orig.tar.gz

The release key is `01282FB9C23478CF97A8D9041727776DA3AF9DF9`; its public
half is `docs/release_public.asc`. Check the fingerprint against a second source
rather than trusting a key file that arrived with the release.

Use `git archive`, not `tar czf`. `export-ignore` in `.gitattributes` is an
attribute `git archive` consults and nothing else does; a tarball rolled by
copying a working tree silently reincludes `extra/`, which carries third-party
GPL-2 xtables-addons source that `debian/copyright` does not account for.

`debian/source/format` is `3.0 (quilt)` rather than `3.0 (native)`, and the
distinction is not cosmetic here. `dpkg-source` does not read `.gitignore`: a
native build over a git working tree would tar `private/` and `scripts/`,
neither of which is tracked and neither of which may be published. Building
from the orig tarball above removes the question entirely — those paths are not
in git, so `git archive` cannot emit them.

Dependencies, and the buildd problem
------------------------------------

`Cargo.lock` has 307 entries — 306 dependencies plus xtgeoip itself — and none
of them come from Debian. Two ways to feed the build:

  * **Network available** (a local build, or a build machine you control).
    Nothing more to do. `debian/rules` resolves over the network.

  * **No network** (a real buildd, and `sbuild`/`pbuilder` by default). Vendor
    the dependencies into a second orig tarball, a `3.0 (quilt)` component
    named `vendor`, before building:

        cd xtgeoip-0.4.3
        cargo vendor --locked vendor
        tar --sort=name --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner \
            -czf ../xtgeoip_0.4.3.orig-vendor.tar.gz vendor
        dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

    `dpkg-source` records both tarballs in the `.dsc` and unpacks the second
    into `vendor/`. `debian/rules` detects that directory, adds `--offline` to
    both the build and the test step, and writes the source-replacement config
    under `debian/cargo_home/` so that nothing outside `debian/` changes.

    Running `cargo vendor` in the unpacked tree *without* rolling the
    component does not work. `vendor/` is then a change to upstream source
    that no orig tarball accounts for, and `dpkg-source` stops with
    "unrepresentable changes to source" before anything compiles.

    The vendor tree is about 300 MB unpacked and 50 MB as the gzipped
    component, and it is part of the source package: `debian/copyright` has to
    account for all 306 crates in it — see the `Comment:` field there.

    This path was built end to end on 2026-09-29 from an unpacked `.dsc`, with
    `CARGO_HTTP_PROXY` pointed at a closed port so that any network access by
    cargo would fail rather than quietly succeed. That build is also why
    `debian/rules` overrides `dh_clean`: every vendored crate ships a
    `Cargo.toml.orig` that its checksum file lists, `dh_clean` deletes `*.orig`
    everywhere by default, and without the override cargo refuses `vendor/`.

The build is `cargo build --release --locked`. The `--locked` is load-bearing
and not a style preference: six crates on the credential path (`argon2`,
`chacha20poly1305`, `secrecy`, `zeroize`, `toml_edit`, `serde-saphyr`) are
exact-pinned because an encrypted blob sits in `/etc/xtgeoip.conf`
indefinitely, in whatever format those crates produced on the day it was
written. A version bump there breaks no build and fails no test; it makes every
existing blob undecryptable. That is also why this recipe does not use
`dh --buildsystem=cargo`, which re-resolves by design.

`rust-toolchain.toml` pins 1.98.0. Debian's `cargo` is not rustup and ignores
that file, which is what you want on a build machine. If you test-build with a
rustup toolchain in the source directory it will honour the pin and fetch
1.98.0 — expected, not a fault of this recipe. `Build-Depends` asks for
`rustc (>= 1.89)` because that is the highest `rust-version` any locked crate
declares (aes 0.9.3, through zip), and cargo refuses to build a crate with an
older compiler than it declares. It is not a tested MSRV, and upstream
declares none. Until 2026-10-04 this said 1.85, edition 2024's floor, which a
1.85 compiler would have disproved at the first `cargo build`; Portage's QA
check found it. `contrib/README.md`, finding 12, has the command that
recomputes it.

That floor is met by Debian unstable, which the changelog targets (rustc
1.95.0 in sid per sources.debian.org, 2026-09-29), and not by Ubuntu 24.04.
There apt's `rustc` is 1.75; the versioned `rustc-1.85`, `-1.89` and `-1.91`
packages install suffixed binaries and do not `Provide: rustc`; and the one
package that does, `rustc-1.74`, provides it at 1.74. Nothing in that archive
satisfies the line. Building there with a rustup toolchain needs
`dpkg-buildpackage -d`, because dpkg cannot see what rustup installed. That is
a mismatch with the host, not with the recipe; do not loosen `Build-Depends`
to hide it.

Tests
-----

`debian/rules` runs `cargo test`, the hermetic suite: no root and no network.
`DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck` skips it.

A proxy in the build environment does not affect it. The tests that talk to
an HTTP stub on 127.0.0.1 build their client with proxies disabled, in test
builds only, so the installed binary still honours `http_proxy` and friends.
Checked on 2026-09-30 by running `cargo test` with every proxy variable pointed
at a closed port.

The suite also writes nothing into the source tree, which matters more here
than it looks: under `3.0 (quilt)` anything a test left behind would surface on
the second `dpkg-source` pass as an unexplained change to upstream source, and
fail the build in a way that reads like a packaging bug. Checked rather than
assumed — `cargo test` followed by `git status --porcelain` leaves the tree
clean. `debian/rules clean` removes the only two things a build does create,
`debian/cargo_home/` and `target/`.

It does *not* run `xtgeoip-tests`, the integration suite, and should not: that
one needs root, a release build, `openssl(1)`, and it prompts for the
credentials passphrase on a terminal once per remote case.

Known deviations from what lintian expects
------------------------------------------

Measured with lintian 2.117.0ubuntu1.5 (Ubuntu 24.04) on 2026-09-30:
`lintian -EvIL +pedantic` against the `.changes` of a build from this recipe,
under both the `debian` and the `ubuntu` profile. Every tag it still reports is
expected, and each is listed here so that nobody chases it:

  * `extra-license-file` (I). `/usr/share/doc/xtgeoip/LICENSE` duplicated
    `debian/copyright`, because the manifest declared it as a file at that
    path and this recipe installs what the manifest declares. Reported by
    that run, and expected to be gone from a build of v0.4.4 or later: the
    licence is now a `license` row with no path, for which `rules` installs
    nothing. Not re-measured; lintian has not been run since the change.
  * `initial-upload-closes-no-bugs` (W). There is no ITP to close; see the top
    of this file.
  * `override_dh_auto_test-does-not-check-DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS` (I). A false
    positive: at debhelper compat 13, `dh` omits the whole override itself
    under `nocheck`. A real `DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck debian/rules build`
    printed `The call to "debian/rules override_dh_auto_test" was omitted due
    to "DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck"`. The tag's own description concedes this
    for compat 13.
  * `newer-standards-version 4.7.4 (current is 4.6.2)` (W). That lintian
    knows Policy only up to 4.6.2; see below.
  * `debian-watch-file-is-missing` (I). None is shipped yet.
  * `upstream-metadata-file-is-missing` and
    `very-long-line-length-in-source-file` (X, both experimental). Pedantic;
    the long lines are paragraphs of Markdown prose.
  * `bad-distribution-in-changes-file unstable` (E), under the `ubuntu`
    profile only. The changelog targets Debian unstable. For an Ubuntu upload,
    change the distribution.

Two tags from an earlier run the same day, against the recipe before these
changes, were acted on:

  * `copyright-without-copyright-notice`. lintian wants a four-digit year
    after `Copyright`. `debian/copyright` and upstream `LICENSE` now carry 2026.
  * `newer-standards-version 4.7.0 (current is 4.6.2)`. Misleading: that
    lintian knows Policy only up to 4.6.2, while sid had Policy 4.7.4 and
    lintian 2.141 on 2026-09-30, where 4.7.0 counts as out of date.
    `Standards-Version` is now 4.7.4, after the 4.7.1–4.7.4 upgrading
    checklist. Nothing installs under `/bin`, `/sbin` or `/lib`; no package in
    sid ships a file named `xtgeoip`, so there is no clash on `PATH`; the
    program does not read its own man page. The lintian here cannot check
    4.7.4, so run a current one before trusting that line.

`Priority: optional` stays, although 4.7.3 no longer recommends stating the
default and sid's lintian tags it `redundant-priority-optional-field`
(pedantic). dpkg supplies the default only from 1.22.12. Without the field, a
build here with dpkg 1.22.6 produced a `.deb` with no Priority at all, and
lintian warned `recommended-field` twice. A pedantic tag on sid is the cheaper
of the two.

Upstream ships no `lintian-overrides`, even for the tags confirmed above. Tag
names change between lintian versions — 2.117 itself records
`initial-upload-closes-no-bugs` as renamed from
`new-package-should-close-itp-bug` — and 2.117 is not the lintian an upload is
checked with. If you upload, add overrides for the tags your lintian emits.

No `xtgeoip-dbgsym` is produced. `debian/rules` explains why in the comment
above `override_dh_strip`, and how to get one.

Cross-building is not handled. `debian/rules` calls `cargo build` without
`--target`, so `dpkg-buildpackage -a<arch>` will produce a host-architecture
binary. Native builds on each architecture work.

Keeping this in step with upstream
----------------------------------

`debian/changelog`'s version has to be bumped alongside `Cargo.toml`'s. That is
a restatement, and it is the only one in this recipe; it is pinned by
`tests::debian_changelog_version_matches_crate` in `src/bin/xtgeoip-docgen.rs`,
which fails the build if the two drift.

Everything else about what gets installed comes from
`docs/generated/install-manifest.tsv` and needs no edit here when the install
set changes.
